+# Executable instantiation -- E-site scenario
+
+Machine simulated for 240 steps of 30 s; 84 steps below freezing.
+
+## Q-a  Policy-driven switching vs. static baselines
+
+| configuration | compute units | accuracy error | switches |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| always coarse (baseline) | 240 | 84 | 0 |
+| always detailed (baseline) | 1920 | 0 | 0 |
+| **policy-driven** | 838 | 10 | 2 |
+
+Policy-driven switching uses 56.4% less compute than always-detailed while cutting the accuracy error of always-coarse by 88.1% (residual error is the held-for dwell before the first refinement).
+
+## Q-b  Runtime overhead of policy interpretation
+
+Interpreting the two-policy set costs 5.5 us per simulation step (median of 5 runs, each averaged over 240 steps; samples: 5.4, 5.6, 5.5, 5.6, 5.5). The toy simulation step here is almost empty, so a ratio against it is uninformative; in absolute terms this is about 0.55% of a single 1 ms simulation step and negligible for any simulation whose step performs real computation.
+
+## Q-a'  Sensitivity of the headline numbers
+
+| cost ratio (detailed:coarse) | trace | compute saving | error reduction | switches |
+|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| 4:1 | single-cold-window | 47.7% | 88.1% | 2 |
+| 4:1 | double-cold-window | 41.0% | 80.4% | 4 |
+| 8:1 | single-cold-window | 56.4% | 88.1% | 2 |
+| 8:1 | double-cold-window | 49.3% | 80.4% | 4 |
+| 16:1 | single-cold-window | 60.7% | 88.1% | 2 |
+| 16:1 | double-cold-window | 53.4% | 80.4% | 4 |
+
+The compute saving grows with the cost ratio (the more expensive the detailed model, the more the policy saves) and the error reduction is trace-shaped (each cold window incurs one held-for dwell); the qualitative result -- policy-driven switching dominates both static baselines -- holds across all variants.
+
+## Q-c  Oscillation ablation: what removes the thrashing?
+
+| policy set | well-formed? | switches on boundary-hover input |
+|---|---|---:|
+| naive (shared boundary, no dwell, no limit) | no (WF4) | 38 |
+| naive + cooldown only (ablation) | -- | 12 |
+| E-site (dwell + hysteresis gap + cooldown) | yes | 0 |
+
 The naive set -- which the validator rejects for violating WF4 -- thrashes at the boundary. Adding only a cooldown (the minimal Execution.Limit) caps the switching rate but cannot eliminate the alternation, because a cooldown is per policy and the two antagonistic policies take turns; the full well-formed set, whose TemporalCondition dwell and hysteresis gap keep the pair out of the contested band entirely, eliminates switching on this input. The statically detected oscillation risk is thus a real runtime pathology, and rate limiting and hysteretic conditions remove distinct parts of it.
